# 073.BondedStages - the two bonded models, side by side

The normal way to work up a BioSpring model: start from an articulated rigid
body, then add real torsion wells when you want realism. This example builds
both so you can look at them together.

```bash
make prep       # builds stage1.nc, stage2.nc
make compare    # point energies of the two, side by side
make run-stage2 # interactive session on either one
```

| stage | `pdb2spn` options added | what becomes real |
|-------|------------------------|-------------------|
| 1 | `--rigidbody` | nothing -- every spring is the uniform `--stiffness` |
| 2 | `--bondedinteraction --dihedral` | torsions (phi/psi/omega, chi1-5, rotors, impropers) |

`make compare` on ubiquitin, both at the same input conformation:

```
stage            spring     dihedral
1                  0.00         0.00
2                  0.00      3009.46
```

Stage 1 reads zero because a rigid-body spring's equilibrium *is* the
distance measured when the structure was loaded -- the input conformation is
the model's own minimum, by construction. Stage 2 adds one channel and
leaves that one untouched, which is the point: the difference between the
two rows is exactly what the dihedral model contributes.


## Bonds and angles are the mesh's business, not a force field's

There is no third stage. Real bond and angle springs used to exist as their
own families (`--stretching` / `--bending`) and were removed, because they
cannot coexist with the dihedral rings: give a bond its physical AMBER
stiffness and the rings deform it.

The reason is in the rings' forces. AMBER's torsion term is a function of
the dihedral angle alone, so its gradient on a substituent atom is purely
tangential -- measured on ubiquitin's Arg42, radial and axial components are
zero to machine precision. A ghost ring is a set of distance springs pulling
along lines that are not tangential, so on the same atom it puts 77 kJ/mol/A
radially and 21 axially against 8 tangentially: about ten times more
parasitic force than useful torque, and the parasitic part never vanishes,
not even where the torsion is at its minimum and AMBER exerts nothing at
all.

The consequence, quenched 6 ps on this same ubiquitin, AMBER's own judgement
of the resulting geometry:

```
    bonds and angles held by       bonds     angles    dihedral
    AMBER stiffnesses             740.94     890.26     -955.08
    the mesh at --stiffness       447.81     344.91    +1272.25
    (the input structure itself)  443.69     278.83
```

in kJ/mol. With physical valence stiffness the geometry ends up strained and
the reported dihedral energy goes negative -- which cannot happen for a term
that reproduces AMBER, since a sum of k(1 + cos(n.phi - gamma)) with k > 0 is
non-negative by construction. Holding bonds and angles in the rigid mesh
instead protects them, and the torsions still relax, which is what the model
is for.


## --stiffness has to be high now

The mesh is the only thing holding bonds and angles, so 250 no longer works.
Same quench, sweeping it:

```
    --stiffness     bonds    angles   dt max
    250          29211.52  26949.30   2.0 fs
    2500           968.37    729.06   0.5 fs
    8000           447.81    344.91   1.0 fs
    25000          424.96    287.52   0.5 fs
```

8000 is what this example uses: the geometry is back to its input quality
(447.81 against 443.69) while a 1 fs step is still stable. dt scales as
1/sqrt(k) -- verified exactly, sqrt(25000/2800) = 2.99 against a measured
ratio of 3.0 -- so nothing surprising happens between those rows.


## Stages are a BUILD-time choice

Two `.nc` files, not one `.nc` and two `.msp` files. That is not laziness,
and it is worth being explicit about because the `.msp` has
`dihedralphi.enable` / … switches that suggest otherwise.

Those switches **isolate a family's contribution inside a given build; they
do not undo the model.** A family not requested at build time never gets a
spring, a ghost particle or a NetCDF entry, so no `.msp` key can bring it
back -- and turning every switch off on `stage2.nc` gives you a rigid body
carrying 2999 dead ghost particles, not `stage1.nc`.

Use the `.msp` switches for what they are good at instead: measuring what one
family costs inside one model, e.g. `dihedralchi.enable = 0` on stage 2 to
see the side chains' share of the 3009.46 above.


## What stage 2 buys

Bonds and angles stay stiff and uniform -- so the geometry cannot drift and
the timestep stays comfortable -- while the torsions carry real AMBER wells,
which is where the conformational energy actually lives. Against AMBER on
the same conformation: 3009.46 kJ/mol here against 3048.83, 1.3 % apart; and
per-axis torque agreement is 0.993 with a 0.69 kJ/mol/rad mean error against
a 9.03 RMS, with zero sign flips across 282 significant axes.


## Files

* `ubiquitin.pdb` -- the structure (1D3Z, NMR, all-atom with hydrogens; 18 of
  the 20 standard residues, Cys and Trp missing).
* `model.msp` -- shared by both stages, deliberately: any difference in the
  reported energies then comes from the model and never from the run
  settings.
* `point.msp` -- same, with `timestep = 0` and one step, so `make compare`
  reads a point energy on the input conformation rather than a relaxed one.
* `model.vmd` -- visualization of stage 2's spring network.
